    "q-363840": {
      "dom_id": "step-q363840",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below. From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>\n        <table cellpadding=\"0\">\n          <tbody>\n            <tr>\n              <td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"right\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"90px\" align=\"center\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n          </tbody>\n        </table>\n      </td>\n      <td>\n        <table cellpadding=\"0\">\n          <tbody>\n            <tr>\n              <td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"right\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"90px\" align=\"center\">\n                $0$\n              </td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n          </tbody>\n        </table>\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
